From: mjb-is <mjb-is@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:36:46 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 083/108] Restore: put back every file time, also 1970 and
 earlier, and folder times

ExtractWithRewriter set a restored file's mtime only when it was greater
than 0. The archive stores seconds since 1970-01-01 as a signed value, so
a file dated exactly 1970-01-01 00:00:00 (0) or any time before 1970
(negative; the writer already records these correctly) came back with
the time of the restore instead. These dates are common on old archives,
camera files and files copied off media that did not keep times.

Folders were never given their time at all: every restored folder showed
the moment of the restore, because writing files into a folder updates
its mtime.

- PXARTreeEntry.ModTimeSet says the entry carried a time, so 0 no longer
  doubles as "none". walk sets it when it decodes the entry.
- Files: any recorded time is applied.
- Folders: their times are collected during the walk and applied after
  it, once nothing more is written into them. Only folders inside the
  selection get the snapshot's time; ancestors a selective restore merely
  creates to reach the selection do not. A folder that already exists
  keeps its own time unless the restore may overwrite.
- pathSelected is pathMatches without the ancestor case.

Tests (pxar_mtime_test.go): 1960 and 1970-01-01 file times, folder
times after a full restore (including an empty folder), ancestors of a
selection left alone, an existing folder with and without overwrite,
and ModTimeSet on listed entries. Against the current reader the first
four fail (files and folders get the restore time); with the fix the
whole pbscommon suite passes on Windows 10 and Linux (Mint 21.3). Vet
clean for pbscommon and gui on windows and linux.

